---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';

/*
 * DRAFT COPY. The structure is settled (see docs/roadmaps/website.md in the
 * planning repo); the wording is a first pass to argue with, not finished text.
 *
 * The one thing that should survive editing: the curl runs before the reader is
 * asked to believe anything. It is a real query against a real venue's pod,
 * unauthenticated, and it takes ten seconds.
 */

const curl = `curl -X POST https://sempods.org/aaltra/_system/sparql/query \\
  -H "Content-Type: application/sparql-query" \\
  -H "Accept: application/sparql-results+json" \\
  --data 'PREFIX schema: <https://schema.org/>
SELECT ?e ?name ?start WHERE {
  ?e a schema:Event ; schema:name ?name ; schema:startDate ?start
} ORDER BY ?start LIMIT 3'`;
---

<Base
  title="sempods — apps come to your data"
  description="A specification for personal data pods: self-hosted spaces where information lives as structured, linked data, readable by any app you permit."
  schemaType="WebSite"
>
  <h1>Apps come to your data</h1>

  <p class="lede">
    A pod is a data space you host. Information lives in it as structured, linked
    data — so any application that speaks the standards can read it, with your
    permission, without anyone building an integration first.
  </p>

  <p>
    That claim is easy to make and hard to believe, so it is worth checking before
    reading further. The command below queries a live pod. No account, no API key,
    no SDK.
  </p>

  <pre><code>{curl}</code></pre>

  <p>
    Those are real events, published by a venue in Chemnitz from a pod it runs
    itself. Nobody granted access for this — the calendar is in a context marked
    public, and public means readable.
  </p>

  <hr class="divider" />

  <h2>The same response, read three ways</h2>

  <p>
    Everything sempods claims is visible in what just came back. It is one example,
    turned three times.
  </p>

  <div class="proof">
    <h3>It is structured, not formatted</h3>
    <p>
      The response says <code>schema:Event</code> and <code>schema:startDate</code>
      — terms from schema.org, not from sempods. A program that has never heard of
      this venue knows it is looking at an event and which field is the date.
    </p>
    <p class="evidence">
      No integration was built for this. Understanding is a property of the data,
      not of an agreement between two vendors.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="proof">
    <h3>It is linked</h3>
    <p>
      That is what <code>?e</code> is doing in the query: every row comes back with
      the event's own URI, something like
      <code>sempods.org/aaltra/events/daokmoft62r6ks3rmalu0hdaao</code>. Request it
      and you get the resource — the venue, the address, whatever else was
      published. Following a link is the same operation as the first request.
    </p>
    <p class="evidence">
      This is Linked Open Data as specified, not an export format. The data is on
      the web rather than behind an API that describes it.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="proof">
    <h3>It is decentralised</h3>
    <p>
      Point the same query at a different pod, run by someone else, and it works.
      There is no registry in the middle, no index that has to know about the venue
      first, and nothing to sign up to.
    </p>
    <p class="evidence">
      The venue can move its pod, and its data keeps working. That is the
      difference between hosting and being hosted.
    </p>
  </div>

  <hr class="divider" />

  <h2>What holds it together</h2>

  <p>
    One thing in that example is invisible: why the calendar is readable and the
    venue's other data is not. That is a <strong>context</strong> — the single
    primitive sempods uses for authorisation. Every statement belongs to exactly
    one, permissions are granted per context, and the query sandbox is enforced by
    the server rather than trusted from the client.
  </p>

  <p>
    There is no second policy language, and no special case that appears once the
    model gets complicated. <a href="/how-it-works">How it works</a> is one page,
    because there is one concept.
  </p>

  <hr class="divider" />

  <h2>What this is not yet</h2>

  <p>
    sempods is early. The specification and the reference implementation are
    public, the artifacts resolve from Maven Central, and real venues publish live
    data from their pods. What does not exist yet is an ecosystem — client
    libraries beyond the JVM, hosted pods anyone can sign up for, applications
    other than the ones built to prove the model.
  </p>

  <p>
    Whether this becomes something depends on people finding the idea worth
    arguing with. Hard questions are the useful contribution right now, more than
    code. <a href="https://github.com/sempods/sempods-kotlin/discussions">The
    discussions</a> are open, and <a href="mailto:hello@sempods.org">hello@sempods.org</a>
    reaches a person.
  </p>

  <p>
    <a href="/start">Start here</a> if you would rather try it than read about it.
  </p>
</Base>
